  "synthetic_dataset_notes": {
    "is_synthetic": true,
    "generation_mechanism": "100% synthetic dataset with independent uniform features (max pairwise |r| < 0.03). 8,642/10,000 rows (86.42%) follow an exact 6-rule deterministic cascade: (1) N < 60 -> Urea; (2) P < 40 -> DAP; (3) K < 40 -> MOP; (4) pH < 5.5 -> Compost; (5) pH > 7.8 -> Zinc Sulphate; (6) Crop_Growth_Stage == 'Vegetative' -> NPK. The remaining 1,358 rows (13.58%) are sampled approximately uniformly across all 7 classes.",
    "bayes_ceiling_19_features": 0.8852,
    "bayes_ceiling_9_features": 0.883,
    "bayes_ceiling_9_features_rules_only_approx": 0.84,
    "accuracy_caveat": "Because Crop_Growth_Stage is excluded from the 9 SoilSense-aligned production features and the 1,358 fallback rows are irreducible uniform label noise, the 9-feature pipeline loses rule #6 (Crop_Growth_Stage == 'Vegetative' -> NPK): pure deterministic-rule coverage drops from 86.42% to 81.89% (~84% if predicting uniformly on fallback, or ~88.3% Bayes ceiling when defaulting the non-rule region to NPK, which drops NPK precision to ~0.33). Do NOT overstate overall accuracy as real-world agronomic accuracy; the model reproduces the synthetic training distribution rather than field trial outcomes.",
    "excluded_feature_rationale": "10 columns (Organic_Carbon, Humidity, Rainfall, Crop_Growth_Stage, Season, Irrigation_Type, Previous_Crop, Region, Fertilizer_Used_Last_Season, Yield_Last_Season) are excluded from the production 9-feature pipeline because SoilSense ESP32 hardware and soil sample workflows only supply NPK, pH, EC, moisture, temperature, crop type, and soil type. Excluding unavailable fields ensures production inference never fabricates missing sensor values."
